        "subheading": "",
        "title": "<p><strong>How They Are Made</strong></p>",
        "description": "<p>Laboratory Grown Diamonds are formed using two primary methods:</p><ul><li><strong>High Pressure High Temperature (HPHT)</strong></li><li><strong>Chemical Vapour Deposition (CVD)</strong> </li></ul><p>At Natalie Marie Jewellery, we offer both, ensuring all stones are certified by either the International Gemmological Institute (IGI) or the Gemmological Institute of America (GIA).<br/>The journey of a laboratory grown diamond is complex and multi-staged:<br/></p><ol><li>Grown in a laboratory</li><li>Cut and polished (often in a separate facility)</li><li>Certified by an independent gemological institute</li><li>Sold through the global market</li><li>Selected by a jeweller<br/></li></ol><p>Like natural diamonds, this journey can involve multiple stages and locations, which can impact traceability.<br/></p>",
        "colour_style": "dark",
        "button_1_text": "",
        "button_1_link": "#",
        "button_style": "underline"
      }
    },
    "content_list_DBeatw": {
      "type": "content-list",
      "blocks": {
        "image_7kfQfe": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "apply_overlay": false,
            "devices": "mobile_desktop",
            "content_position": "middleLeft",
            "subheading": "",
            "title": "<p><strong>Sustainability Considerations</strong></p>",
            "description": "<p>Key considerations include:<br/></p><ul><li><strong>Energy intensive production  </strong><br/>Diamond growth requires extremely high temperatures and sustained energy input.</li><li><strong>Energy source matters<br/></strong> A large proportion of global production occurs in regions reliant on coal powered electricity.</li><li><strong>Water usage </strong><br/>Significant water is required for cooling systems during production.</li><li><strong>Raw material inputs <br/></strong>Despite being “grown”, these diamonds still rely on mined diamond 'seeds'.<br/></li></ul><p>For a laboratory grown diamond to be considered more sustainable, it would require:</p><ul><li>Renewable energy sources</li><li>Transparent supply chains</li><li>Verified carbon reduction or offsetting<br/></li></ul><p>At present, industry wide transparency remains limited, making these claims difficult to fully validate.</p>",
            "description_mobile": "<p>Key considerations include:<br/></p><ul><li><strong>Energy intensive production  </strong><br/>Diamond growth requires extremely high temperatures and sustained energy input.</li><li><strong>Energy source matters<br/></strong> A large proportion of global production occurs in regions reliant on coal powered electricity.</li><li><strong>Water usage </strong><br/>Significant water is required for cooling systems during production.</li><li><strong>Raw material inputs <br/></strong>Despite being “grown”, these diamonds still rely on mined diamond 'seeds'.<br/></li></ul><p>For a laboratory grown diamond to be considered more sustainable, it would require:</p><ul><li>Renewable energy sources</li><li>Transparent supply chains</li><li>Verified carbon reduction or offsetting<br/></li></ul><p>At present, industry wide transparency remains limited, making these claims difficult to fully validate.</p>",
            "colour_style": "dark",
